          {
            "caption": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "Movement of carbon dioxide through tissues and blood cells involves several equilibrium reactions."
                  }
                ],
                "type": "p"
              }
            ],
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "frt31kh5qco6abb",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "tkcrm0wz0t08oa4",
            "src": "https://d2xvti2irp4c7t.cloudfront.net/media/7b/7b3a54718964643bf4e2d20d640fbdc8/equil_intro.jpg",
            "type": "img",
            "width": "650"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": ""
              }
            ],
            "id": "9sq71tz1ixact0i",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Imagine a beach populated with sunbathers and swimmers. As those basking in the sun get too hot and want to cool off, they head into the surf to swim. As the swimmers tire, they head to the beach to rest. If these two rates of transfer (sunbathers entering the water, swimmers leaving the water) are equal, the number of sunbathers and swimmers would be constant, or at equilibrium, although the identities of the people are constantly changing from sunbather to swimmer and back. An analogous situation occurs in chemical reactions. Reactions can occur in both directions simultaneously (reactants to products and products to reactants) and eventually reach a state of balance."
              }
            ],
            "id": "1637111281",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "These balanced two-way reactions occur all around and even in us. For example, they occur in our blood, where the reaction between carbon dioxide and water forms carbonic acid (H"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": "CO"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "3"
              },
              {
                "text": "). Human physiology is adapted to the amounts of the ionized products produced by this reaction (HCO"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "3"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "−"
              },
              {
                "text": " and H"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "+"
              },
              {
                "text": "). This unit provides a thorough introduction to the essential aspects of chemical equilibria."
              }
            ],
            "id": "jivamauvw9qam5c",
            "type": "p"
          },
